      "trimesterTwo_title": "Second Trimester",
      "@trimesterTwo_title": {
        "description": "Title for the second trimester page"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_header": "Second Trimester of Pregnancy",
      "@trimesterTwo_header": {
        "description": "Header title for the second trimester page"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_intro": "The second trimester lasts from the 14th week to the 27th week. During this period, the fetus experiences rapid growth, and the symptoms felt by the mother are usually lighter compared to the first trimester. This is also the time when many pregnant mothers start feeling fetal movement for the first time.",
      "@trimesterTwo_intro": {
        "description": "Intro paragraph explaining the second trimester"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_weeklyDevelopment_title": "Weekly Fetal Development",
      "@trimesterTwo_weeklyDevelopment_title": {
        "description": "Title for the weekly fetal development section"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_week14_15": "Weeks 14-15",
      "@trimesterTwo_week14_15": {
        "description": "Label for weeks 14-15"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_week14_15_desc": "Fetal size: About 9-10 cm and weighing around 70-85 grams. The fetus''s face is becoming more developed, with eyes and ears starting to move to more proper positions. The baby starts moving hands and feet, though the mother may not feel these movements yet.",
      "@trimesterTwo_week14_15_desc": {
        "description": "Description of fetal development in weeks 14-15"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_week16_17": "Weeks 16-17",
      "@trimesterTwo_week16_17": {
        "description": "Label for weeks 16-17"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_week16_17_desc": "Fetal size: About 12-14 cm and weighing around 100-150 grams. The fetal heart begins to pump about 25 liters of blood daily. Fingerprints begin to form, and a layer of fat starts developing under the skin. By the end of week 16, some mothers may feel the first fetal movements known as 'quickening'.",
      "@trimesterTwo_week16_17_desc": {
        "description": "Description of fetal development in weeks 16-17"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_week18_19": "Weeks 18-19",
      "@trimesterTwo_week18_19": {
        "description": "Label for weeks 18-19"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_week18_19_desc": "Fetal size: About 15-18 cm and weighing around 200-250 grams. The fetus's ears can hear sounds from outside, including the mother's voice. Vernix, a protective waxy layer, begins to cover the fetal skin to protect it from amniotic fluid.",
      "@trimesterTwo_week18_19_desc": {
        "description": "Description of fetal development in weeks 18-19"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_week20_21": "Weeks 20-21",
      "@trimesterTwo_week20_21": {
        "description": "Label for weeks 20-21"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_week20_21_desc": "Fetal size: About 20-25 cm and weighing around 300-400 grams. All major organs are formed and continue to develop. Eyebrows and scalp hair begin to grow, and the fetus''s first teeth start developing beneath the gums. Mothers usually begin to feel more regular fetal movements.",
      "@trimesterTwo_week20_21_desc": {
        "description": "Description of fetal development in weeks 20-21"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_week22_23": "Weeks 22-23",
      "@trimesterTwo_week22_23": {
        "description": "Label for weeks 22-23"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_week22_23_desc": "Fetal size: About 25-28 cm and weighing around 450-500 grams. The fetus's eyes are fully formed but still closed. The sense of taste starts to develop, and the fetus can hear the mother's heartbeat and external sounds.",
      "@trimesterTwo_week22_23_desc": {
        "description": "Description of fetal development in weeks 22-23"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_week24_25": "Weeks 24-25",
      "@trimesterTwo_week24_25": {
        "description": "Label for weeks 24-25"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_week24_25_desc": "Fetal size: About 28-30 cm and weighing around 600-700 grams. The fetal lungs start producing surfactant, a substance that will aid in breathing after birth. The central nervous system is further developing, and the fetus begins to regulate sleep patterns.",
      "@trimesterTwo_week24_25_desc": {
        "description": "Description of fetal development in weeks 24-25"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_week26_27": "Weeks 26-27",
      "@trimesterTwo_week26_27": {
        "description": "Label for weeks 26-27"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_week26_27_desc": "Fetal size: About 30-35 cm and weighing around 800-1000 grams. The fetus begins to open its eyes and respond to light entering through the uterus. Fetal movements become more regular and strong, and the mother may feel fetal hiccups.",
      "@trimesterTwo_week26_27_desc": {
        "description": "Description of fetal development in weeks 26-27"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_motherChanges_title": "Changes in the Mother",
      "@trimesterTwo_motherChanges_title": {
        "description": "Title for the section on changes in the mother"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_motherChanges_14_16": "Weeks 14-16: Mothers may begin feeling more energetic, and morning sickness symptoms (nausea) may lessen. The belly starts to expand, and some mothers may start wearing maternity clothes. Increased appetite may also occur.",
      "@trimesterTwo_motherChanges_14_16": {
        "description": "Changes in the mother during weeks 14-16"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_motherChanges_17_20": "Weeks 17-20: The growing uterus begins pressing on other organs in the abdomen, causing the mother to urinate more frequently and sometimes experience shortness of breath. Skin changes may occur, such as the appearance of the 'linea nigra', a dark line down the middle of the belly that will fade after childbirth.",
      "@trimesterTwo_motherChanges_17_20": {
        "description": "Changes in the mother during weeks 17-20"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_motherChanges_21_24": "Weeks 21-24: At this stage, some mothers may start experiencing back pain or leg cramps due to weight gain and changes in posture. The breasts also begin to produce colostrum, the first milk that will nourish the baby.",
      "@trimesterTwo_motherChanges_21_24": {
        "description": "Changes in the mother during weeks 21-24"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_motherChanges_25_27": "Weeks 25-27: Mothers may experience mild swelling in the ankles and hands due to fluid retention. Some may also experience 'sciatica', pain in the sciatic nerve caused by pressure from the growing uterus.",
      "@trimesterTwo_motherChanges_25_27": {
        "description": "Changes in the mother during weeks 25-27"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_commonComplaints_title": "Common Complaints",
      "@trimesterTwo_commonComplaints_title": {
        "description": "Title for the section on common complaints"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_commonComplaints_backPain": "Back Pain: Postural changes and weight gain often cause back pain.",
      "@trimesterTwo_commonComplaints_backPain": {
        "description": "Common complaint related to back pain"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_commonComplaints_legCramps": "Leg Cramps: Leg cramps, especially at night, are common due to changes in circulation and pressure on the leg nerves.",
      "@trimesterTwo_commonComplaints_legCramps": {
        "description": "Common complaint related to leg cramps"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_commonComplaints_swelling": "Mild Swelling: Mothers may experience mild swelling in the hands and feet due to fluid retention, which worsens at night or after prolonged standing.",
      "@trimesterTwo_commonComplaints_swelling": {
        "description": "Common complaint related to mild swelling"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_commonComplaints_constipation": "Constipation and Hemorrhoids: Increased progesterone slows digestion, leading to constipation, which can develop into hemorrhoids.",
      "@trimesterTwo_commonComplaints_constipation": {
        "description": "Common complaint related to constipation and hemorrhoids"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_commonComplaints_breathlessness": "Shortness of Breath: The growing uterus can press on the diaphragm, causing mild breathing difficulties.",
      "@trimesterTwo_commonComplaints_breathlessness": {
        "description": "Common complaint related to shortness of breath"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_dos_title": "What to Do",
      "@trimesterTwo_dos_title": {
        "description": "Title for the 'What to Do' section"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_dos_nutrition": "Eat Nutritious Food: Ensure a diet rich in protein, calcium, and iron to support fetal growth. Eating fiber-rich foods helps prevent constipation.",
      "@trimesterTwo_dos_nutrition": {
        "description": "Advice on proper nutrition"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_dos_hydration": "Drink Plenty of Water: Staying hydrated is important to avoid constipation and prevent excessive swelling.",
      "@trimesterTwo_dos_hydration": {
        "description": "Advice on staying hydrated"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_dos_exercise": "Light Exercise: Engage in safe exercises such as walking, swimming, or prenatal yoga to stay fit and reduce back pain.",
      "@trimesterTwo_dos_exercise": {
        "description": "Advice on light exercise"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_dos_clothing": "Wear Comfortable Clothing: Choose loose, comfortable clothing, including supportive underwear for growing breasts.",
      "@trimesterTwo_dos_clothing": {
        "description": "Advice on wearing comfortable clothing"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_dos_rest": "Get Plenty of Rest: Ensure enough sleep, especially as the baby becomes more active at night.",
      "@trimesterTwo_dos_rest": {
        "description": "Advice on getting sufficient rest"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_donts_title": "What to Avoid",
      "@trimesterTwo_donts_title": {
        "description": "Title for the 'What to Avoid' section"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_donts_heavyLifting": "Avoid Lifting Heavy Objects: Lifting heavy objects can cause back injuries or increase the risk of premature labor.",
      "@trimesterTwo_donts_heavyLifting": {
        "description": "Warning to avoid heavy lifting"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_donts_prolongedStanding": "Avoid Prolonged Standing: Avoid standing or sitting for too long to prevent swelling in the legs and discomfort.",
      "@trimesterTwo_donts_prolongedStanding": {
        "description": "Warning to avoid prolonged standing or sitting"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_donts_medication": "Avoid Taking Medication Without a Prescription: Do not take any medication without consulting a doctor, including over-the-counter drugs such as aspirin.",
      "@trimesterTwo_donts_medication": {
        "description": "Warning against taking medication without a doctor's prescription"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_donts_lyingOnBack": "Avoid Sleeping on Your Back: Avoid sleeping on your back as it may restrict blood flow to the fetus. The best position is to sleep on your left side.",
      "@trimesterTwo_donts_lyingOnBack": {
        "description": "Warning to avoid sleeping on the back"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_donts_ignoringMovement": "Don''t Ignore Fetal Movement: If the mother feels reduced or no fetal movement for a prolonged period, she should contact a doctor immediately for further examination.",
      "@trimesterTwo_donts_ignoringMovement": {
        "description": "Warning not to ignore changes in fetal movement"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_maternalChangesImage_caption": "Physical changes in pregnant mothers during the second trimester",
      "@trimesterTwo_maternalChangesImage_caption": {
        "description": "Caption for the image on physical changes in the mother"
      },
    
      "trimesterTwo_cautionImage_caption": "Things to avoid during the second trimester of pregnancy",
      "@trimesterTwo_cautionImage_caption": {
        "description": "Caption for the image on things to avoid"
      },
    
